WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.010 --> 00:00:02.880
<v Instructor>Okay, so yet another theoretical lecture,</v>

2
00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:04.260
but I still want to mention it

3
00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:05.640
because I think it's extremely important

4
00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:06.720
going to the exam.

5
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:09.600
You need to know about the services that AWS offers

6
00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:11.070
at a very, very high level,

7
00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:13.500
but just hear about them once

8
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:16.320
to do on-premise strategy with the cloud.

9
00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.450
So, we have the ability to download

10
00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:21.150
the Amazon Linux 2 AMI as a virtual machine,

11
00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:23.370
and it will be in the ISO format.

12
00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:25.590
And you can load this ISO image

13
00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:28.680
into the common software used to create VMs.

14
00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:30.930
That includes VMware, KVM,

15
00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:32.760
Virtual Box, which is Oracle VM,

16
00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:34.800
or Microsoft Hyper V.

17
00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:37.140
And this would allow you to run Amazon Linux 2

18
00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:40.470
on your on-premise infrastructure directly using that VM.

19
00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:41.880
That means that you can make it work

20
00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:43.650
with some user data and so on.

21
00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:45.240
So, quite cool to think about.

22
00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:48.270
Then, we have a feature called VM Import/Export,

23
00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:49.590
and what this allows you to do

24
00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:52.860
is to migrate your existing VMs and applications

25
00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:55.200
into EC2 directly using this feature.

26
00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.000
And you can also create a,

27
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:00.060
for example, a disaster recovery repository strategy

28
00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:01.890
if you had a lot of on-premise VMs,

29
00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:04.440
but you wanted to back them up into the cloud.

30
00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:06.960
And because it's called import and export,

31
00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:10.020
you can export back the VMs from EC2

32
00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:13.290
to your on-premise environment if you wanted to.

33
00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:16.140
Now, for AWS Application Discovery Service,

34
00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:18.000
well, this is a service that allows you

35
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.340
to gather information about your on-premise servers

36
00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:21.900
and plan a migration.

37
00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:23.010
This is very high level,

38
00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:25.830
but it does give you some server utilization information

39
00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:27.120
and dependency mappings,

40
00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:28.650
and that could be quite helpful

41
00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:31.350
when you want to do a massive migration

42
00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:33.150
from on-premise to the cloud.

43
00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:35.310
Finally, you can track all that migration

44
00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:38.220
using the AWS Migration Hub.

45
00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:41.430
Then, we have AWS Database Migration Service, or DMS,

46
00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:43.550
which allows you to replicate from on-premise to AWS,

47
00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:47.370
AWS to AWS, or AWS to on-premise for your database.

48
00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:48.203
This is quite nice,

49
00:01:48.203 --> 00:01:50.040
because if you had a MySQL

50
00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:52.080
or a PostgreSQL database running on premise

51
00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:55.230
and you wanted to start moving your workload into AWS,

52
00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:58.260
you could use DMS to replicate the database in the meantime.

53
00:01:58.260 --> 00:01:59.093
And when you're ready,

54
00:01:59.093 --> 00:02:02.070
you can fully transition to using AWS only.

55
00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:02.903
And the nice thing about it

56
00:02:02.903 --> 00:02:05.370
is it works with various database technologies.

57
00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:09.510
That includes Oracle, MySQL, DynamoDB, et cetera,

58
00:02:09.510 --> 00:02:12.600
and allows you to do some really fancy use cases,

59
00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:17.430
for example, migrate data from MySQL into DynamoDB, okay?

60
00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:20.250
And finally, you have the AWS Application Migration Service,

61
00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:21.420
or MGN,

62
00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:23.580
which is used for incremental replication

63
00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:27.120
of on-premises live servers to AWS.

64
00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:28.777
So, at a high level, you may be like,

65
00:02:28.777 --> 00:02:30.510
"Oh, this is a lot of services."

66
00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:32.310
Just remember the names at a high level.

67
00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:35.940
So, Linux 2 on on-premise is possible.

68
00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:39.690
We can do VM import and export for on-premise and EC2.

69
00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:41.370
We can have migration services,

70
00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:43.587
such as Application Discovery Service,

71
00:02:43.587 --> 00:02:45.030
AWS Migration Hub,

72
00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:46.260
Database Migration Service

73
00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:47.580
and Server Migration Service,

74
00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:49.020
DMS and SMS.

75
00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:50.850
And just remember those at a high level, that's it,

76
00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:51.683
just so you can see,

77
00:02:51.683 --> 00:02:53.100
if you see the name in the question,

78
00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:56.100
then you're not taken by surprise, okay?

79
00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:59.580
You know that all these things have to relate to on-premise.

80
00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:02.190
Okay, well, that's for this short theory lecture.

81
00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:03.940
I will see you in the next lecture.

